"Doors and Lamps" by "Tester"

[I7-lite 7b features in one small story: a locked two-sided door, a key,
 a device, articles, printed names, synonyms, presence rules.]

A room is usually dark.
The Depression is a room. "You are in a small depression. Below you is a grate." The Depression is lit.
The grate is a door. It is below the Depression and above the Chamber. The grate is locked.
The Chamber is a room. "A dry chamber, dimly lit by daylight from the grate above."
The set of keys is in the Depression. The keys unlock the grate. The keys are plural-named. The indefinite article of the keys is "some". Understand "key" as the keys.
The lamp is a device in the Depression. The printed name of the lamp is "brass lantern". The lamp is privately-named. Understand "lamp" or "lantern" as the lamp.
Carry out switching on the lamp: now the lamp is lit.
Carry out switching off the lamp: now the lamp is unlit.
The lamp can be lit or unlit.
Understand "plugh" as down. Understand the command "grab" as "take".
The bird is a person in the Chamber.
Instead of waiting in the presence of the bird, say "The bird sings."
Instead of jumping, try looking.
Jumping is an action applying to nothing. Understand "jump" as jumping.
